"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
LESSONS={}
def put(n,raw):
    LESSONS[n]=[]
    for block in raw.strip().split("\n\n"):
        prose,trace=block.strip().split(" || ")
        LESSONS[n].append(dict(prose=prose.replace(" ¶ ","\n\n"),trace=trace.split(" / ")))

put(2, '''Suppose we are asked to build a social feed. Before estimating servers, we need to know what people will do: publish posts, follow accounts, read updates, or report harmful content. Following one of these activities from the user's intention to its result gives us a user journey. Each journey causes different work inside the system. ¶ We might ask the team, “If publishing works but followers cannot see the post, has the feature succeeded?” The answer determines what we must test. A requirement connects a person's intention to an observable result; a feature name such as “posting” leaves too much undecided. ¶ Write the required outcome for each important journey. “Keep an accepted post and show it to followers within five seconds” is specific enough to discuss and test. Then separate essential behaviour from desirable improvements. Investigate requirements whose failure would cause the greatest harm or whose feasibility is uncertain. Record exclusions too: private messaging should not silently enter an estimate for a public feed. || Journey: publish a post / Must: retain accepted post / Target: visible within 5 s / Exclude: direct messages

Opening a feed appears to be one action. In an assumed session, however, the browser sends one feed request, fetches twenty images, and records one analytics event. The page first needs a list of posts, then the images named in that list. An estimate that counts only the page opening misses most of these requests. Drawing this short sequence helps us find the work before putting numbers against it. ¶ A workload describes the mixture of operations and how often they arrive. For each operation, estimate its rate, the size of the data it carries, and the other components it needs. A small response can still require expensive computation; an image can require little computation but substantial transfer. Keep these workloads separate when finding bottlenecks: resources whose limits restrict useful work. The first estimates are assumptions. Replace them with measurements once the application is running. || 1 feed request / 20 image requests / 1 analytics event / Separate workloads and budgets

Assume users read ten feeds for every post they publish. At the public API, this looks read-heavy. But suppose publishing saves one durable post and then copies a reference into a thousand follower feeds. That one action causes a thousand additional storage writes. ¶ Imagine two authors: one has ten followers and another has a million. The same publish button can cause very different amounts of work. Counting posts alone hides this difference. We need both the frequency of the action and the amount of work that each action produces. ¶ A read/write ratio therefore needs a boundary: are we counting user actions, API requests, or database operations? Creating follower feeds in advance moves work to publication time. Building them when requested moves work to reading time. The choice depends on how often users publish and read, as well as the distribution of follower counts. Additional database copies used to serve reads, called read replicas, can help some reads. If their updates are delayed, however, they may not immediately show a user's new post. || 1 publish action / 1 durable post write / 1000 follower-feed writes / Logical ratio differs from storage ratio

Suppose a server receives 200 requests each second and completes 180. If all the remaining requests stay queued, 200 − 180 = 20 more unfinished requests accumulate every second. After ten seconds, about 20 × 10 = 200 requests remain unfinished, assuming an empty starting queue and no rejection or abandonment. Reporting only the completion rate would hide the growing wait. ¶ Latency is the time one operation takes within a stated boundary. From the caller's perspective, it includes waiting as well as execution. Throughput is the amount of work completed per unit time. A server can keep producing the same number of answers each second while users experience progressively longer delays. ¶ RPS means requests per second, QPS queries per second, and TPS transactions per second. State which work each number counts, since one request may cause several queries. Measure offered work, successful completions, errors, and rejected requests together. Otherwise, an apparently steady throughput can conceal users who are waiting or giving up. || Offered: 200 requests/s / Completed: 180 requests/s / Backlog: +20 requests/s / Measure rejection and timeout too

Consider 100 assumed request times: 99 requests take 10 milliseconds each, and one takes 1,000 milliseconds. The first group contributes 99 × 10 = 990 ms. Adding the slow request gives 1,990 ms, so the mean is 1,990/100 = 19.9 ms. That average tells us little about the user who waited a full second. ¶ To find the median, arrange the observations from fastest to slowest and look at the middle. For these 100 observations, the two middle values are both 10 ms, so the median is 10 ms. A percentile names a position in this ordered set. Under the nearest-rank convention, p95 is the 95th value and p99 is the 99th value. Both are 10 ms here: even p99 can miss the single slowest request. ¶ The slow end of the distribution is called its tail. Inspect the distribution and its extreme observations together; percentile estimates also depend on the convention and sample size. To find a fleet-wide p99, combine compatible observations or histograms, which count observations in latency ranges. Averaging individual servers' p99 values does not produce the fleet p99. Account for timed-out requests rather than discarding them. || 99 requests at 10 ms / 1 request at 1000 ms / Mean = 19.9 ms / Tail experience remains 1000 ms

A payment status page can respond quickly while showing yesterday's information. Its ability to respond and the freshness of its answer are separate properties. ¶ Ask four separate questions about the status page. Can it answer now? Is its answer permitted by the reading rules? Will a committed payment survive the failures we promised to tolerate? Does the service keep working correctly over time? Answering one question does not settle the other three. ¶ Availability concerns providing the required service when needed. Durability concerns whether committed data survives specified failures. Reliability usually concerns correct service over time; state how it is being measured. Consistency defines which observations of state are allowed. For example, must the next read show a completed payment? Choose the requirement for each operation. A popularity count may tolerate delayed updates, while selling the last ticket requires stronger coordination. A single label such as “reliable database” leaves these decisions unanswered. || Request responds successfully / Response may be stale / Acknowledged state may be lost / Test each promise separately

Suppose we want to know whether people can use a service successfully. A service-level indicator, or SLI, is a measured property of the service, such as the fraction of eligible requests that succeed. A service-level objective, or SLO, gives that indicator a target and measurement window: for example, 99.9% successful requests over a month. A service-level agreement, or SLA, is an agreement with stated obligations and possibly consequences. ¶ Assume this service handles one million eligible requests in the month. A 99.9% success target leaves 100% − 99.9% = 0.1% for failures. Since 0.1% is 0.001 as a fraction, the allowance is 1,000,000 × 0.001 = 1,000 failed requests. This is its event-based error budget for that population and period. After 600 eligible failures, 1,000 − 600 = 400 remain within the target. ¶ Define eligible requests and successful outcomes before measuring them. Excluding the failures we meant to track would defeat the purpose. A time-based objective uses time as its denominator and gives a different budget. Budget consumption can guide releases and reliability work, but failures concentrated during checkout may require action even while some budget remains. || 1000000 eligible events / Target: 99.9% good / Allowance: 1000 bad events / Track rate of budget consumption

Suppose stored data grows by an assumed 10% each month. Starting with one unit of storage, the first month ends with 1 × 1.1 = 1.1 units. The next ends with 1.1 × 1.1 = 1.21 units. Each increase applies to the new, larger total. After twelve months, repeated multiplication gives 1 × 1.1¹² ≈ 3.14 units. This is compound growth. ¶ The result is a planning scenario, not evidence that request traffic and CPU usage will also triple. Plan each important resource separately: stored history, frequently accessed data, background jobs, and spare capacity during failures. Include temporary space needed while old and new data layouts coexist during a migration. ¶ Suppose storage is likely to fill first. We need to act early enough to add capacity before that happens, including the time needed to purchase, prepare, and move data onto it. A useful capacity plan therefore names the limiting resource, the lead time for expansion, and the observation that triggers action. Revisit those assumptions as measurements arrive. || Starting volume: 1 unit / Monthly multiplier: 1.1 / After 12 months: 3.14 units / Recheck traffic and resource mix

Assume ten million daily users each make fifty API requests. The daily total is 10,000,000 users × 50 requests/user/day = 500,000,000 requests/day. A day contains 24 × 60 × 60 = 86,400 seconds, so the average rate is 500,000,000/86,400 ≈ 5,787 requests per second. ¶ For this daily workload, separately assume that each user publishes one 2 kB post and views ten 200 kB images per day. These daily assumptions differ from the single-session example earlier. Using decimal units, 2 kB is 2,000 bytes, so raw post growth is 10,000,000 × 2,000 = 20,000,000,000 bytes/day, or 20 GB/day. Image delivery is 10,000,000 × 10 × 200,000 = 20,000,000,000,000 bytes/day, or 20 TB/day. ¶ Stored bytes accumulate while they are retained; delivered bytes measure transfer. Spreading 20 TB evenly across the day gives about 231.5 MB/s, or 1.85 gigabits/s because each byte contains eight bits. This average bandwidth excludes protocol overhead and bursts. Add indexes, extra copies, retained history, and transfer overhead to the relevant resource estimates. ¶ Memory depends on the data kept active, including caches and in-flight requests. CPU demand depends on the work each operation performs, which we must measure. Neither follows from the user count alone. These assumed workloads give us inputs for testing, not company benchmarks or a complete server count. || API: 500 million requests/day / Posts: 20 GB raw/day / Images: 20 TB delivered/day / Add indexes, replicas and headroom

A daily average spreads all requests evenly across 24 hours. Real users may arrive together after a notification or event. Suppose, for planning, that peak API traffic is five times the preceding average. The estimate becomes 5 × 5,787.04 ≈ 28,935 requests per second. Five is an assumed multiplier; a measured traffic trace would give us better evidence. ¶ Peak duration matters too. Suppose a spike adds 1,000 requests each second beyond current capacity for 30 seconds. Without rejection or extra completions, it adds 1,000 requests/s × 30 s = 30,000 queued requests. The service must later complete this backlog, so the wait can continue after the incoming rate falls. ¶ A bounded queue can absorb some temporary excess if the resulting wait is acceptable. Existing spare capacity and admission limits must cover the interval before extra instances are ready. Instances that take several minutes to start may arrive after a short spike ends. Image traffic and posting traffic may peak differently, and retries during an outage can add attempts that ordinary user averages miss. || Average: 5787 requests/s / Assumed peak multiplier: 5 / Peak: 28935 requests/s / Scaling delay still needs protection

Imagine a stable service completing 500 operations per second, with arrivals balanced by completions over a sufficiently long period. Each operation spends an average of 40 milliseconds inside the boundary we measure, including any waiting there. We want to estimate how many operations are present inside that boundary at once. ¶ Little's Law relates three averages. Let L be the average number of operations in the system, λ the long-run arrival rate, and W the average time an operation spends inside it. Then L = λW. Convert 40 ms to 0.04 seconds to match the rate: 500 operations/s × 0.04 s = 20 operations. About twenty are present on average, either waiting or being served. This does not mean there are exactly twenty at every moment. ¶ If mean residence time rises to 0.4 seconds while stable throughput remains 500 operations/s, the average number present becomes 500 × 0.4 = 200. Longer residence leaves more work in progress. Use consistent boundaries and units: a rate measured at the API cannot simply be combined with time spent only inside the database. The relation describes stable averages; it neither predicts p99 nor makes a queue with sustained excess arrivals stable. || Arrival/completion rate: 500/s / Mean residence: 0.04 s / Mean work in flight: 20 / At 0.4 s: 200 in flight

Suppose a job takes 100 seconds on one worker. Twenty seconds must run sequentially, meaning that this part cannot be divided among workers. The other eighty seconds can be shared perfectly. With four workers, the shareable part takes 80/4 = 20 seconds. Total time becomes 20 + 20 = 40 seconds, so the ideal speedup is 100/40 = 2.5 times. ¶ This is an application of Amdahl's Law for a fixed amount of work. Expressed as fractions of the original time, the sequential part is 0.2 and the parallel part is 0.8. The new duration is 0.2 + 0.8/4 = 0.4 of the old duration. Even infinitely many workers cannot remove the sequential twenty seconds, giving an ideal ceiling of 100/20 = five times. ¶ The calculation assumes no extra cost from dividing and coordinating the work. Such costs can reduce the real benefit. Find the stage that limits progress before adding workers. After improving it, measure again: another stage may become the new bottleneck. || Serial fraction: 0.2 / Parallel fraction: 0.8 / 4 workers: 2.5x ideal speedup / Infinite workers: 5x ceiling

A page request may start fifty calls in parallel and need all their results. Its completion depends on the slowest required call: forty-nine quick answers cannot compensate for a missing last answer. Making several calls from one request is called fan-out. ¶ Assume each call independently has a 1% chance of exceeding a chosen time threshold. Each therefore has a 99%, or 0.99, chance of finishing within it. Independence lets us multiply these probabilities: the chance that all fifty are fast is 0.99⁵⁰ ≈ 0.605. The remaining probability, 1 − 0.605 ≈ 0.395, means about 39.5% of page requests have at least one slow required call in this model. Shared dependencies can correlate delays, so independence must be justified. ¶ We can sometimes change which calls are required: recommendations might arrive later while the main page appears. For the calls that remain required, divide the overall deadline into a latency budget. The illustrated sequential example allows 30 ms for network travel, 20 ms for waiting, 100 ms for data access, and 50 ms for computation and the response: 200 ms altogether. Real overlap and variability need measurement. Sending duplicate calls to reduce waiting spends extra capacity and requires repeated execution to be safe. || 50 required parallel calls / Per-call slow probability: 0.01 / All fast probability: 0.99^50 / At least one slow: about 39.5%

Suppose two designs both handle the expected traffic. One is cheaper but allows a tenant's cached result to reach another tenant. It fails a security requirement and should be rejected before comparing prices. ¶ Write mandatory requirements as pass-or-fail checks first. “A tenant must never receive another tenant's private result” is one such check. We can then compare cost and ease of operation among the candidates that pass. Otherwise, a cheap but unsuitable candidate can look attractive simply because we assigned a small numerical weight to an essential rule. ¶ Other differences may be preferences: one design costs more money but takes less operating work. Include staffing, maintenance, migration, and recovery in the comparison. A team also needs the skills to run what it builds. Compare candidates against the same workload and failure requirements. A price advantage obtained by dropping backups or weakening isolation represents a different service, rather than a like-for-like saving. || Check mandatory correctness / Check security and operation limits / Estimate total lifecycle cost / Compare feasible candidates

After rejecting designs that fail mandatory requirements, we may still have several reasonable choices. A decision matrix lists the criteria, gives each candidate a score, and weights the criteria according to their importance. It makes the team's judgments visible. ¶ Suppose cost has weight two and ease of operation has weight one. A candidate scoring three on cost and five on operation receives 2 × 3 + 1 × 5 = 6 + 5 = 11. Here a higher score means a more desirable result, so a higher cost score must mean better affordability. Use that same direction in every column and explain how the scores were chosen. The total summarises these judgments; it does not independently establish them. ¶ Try changing uncertain scores and weights. If a small change reverses the result, seek better evidence or prefer a choice that is easier to reverse. Record the choice, reason, and condition for reconsidering it in an architecture decision record, or ADR. Tests are still needed to establish performance and correctness. A high preference score cannot make an unsupported requirement true. || Reject infeasible candidates / Score explicit preferences / Vary uncertain weights / Record choice and revisit trigger''')

put(3, '''Type a website address into a browser. Before the page appears, the browser may look up the name, establish a connection, arrange secure communication, send a request, and fetch additional resources. The server must also perform its application work, and the browser must render the result: turn the response into the page we see. ¶ Take a request for the task list. The browser separates the website name from the requested path, such as /tasks. It obtains a network address for the name, uses a connection to that destination, and sends an HTTP request for the path. HTTP is the application protocol that gives this request and its response a common format and meaning. The application may ask a database for the tasks before replying. The browser reads the reply and updates the page. ¶ Some steps can reuse earlier work. A cached address is a saved lookup result; an existing connection may already be ready to carry another request. Follow the path used by the actual request before assigning a single “network time.” A fast server can still produce a slow page when images are large or connection setup requires several exchanges. We will attach each networking term to a step in this request path. || URL parsed / Name and route resolved / Secure connection used / Response parsed and rendered

To contact a web server, a client needs an IP address and a transport port. The address identifies a network destination. Routing moves packets, the units of data carried by the network, toward that destination. The port helps the destination operating system direct transport traffic to the appropriate endpoint there. ¶ For example, the browser may connect to a server address on port 443, commonly used for HTTPS. The browser also has a local address and port for its side of the connection. A socket is an endpoint the operating system provides for communication. The application reads and writes through it; the operating system associates arriving traffic with the appropriate socket and handles the packet exchange. ¶ An application can listen on port 443 and still be unreachable. It might be bound only to a local address, so remote traffic cannot reach that listener. A firewall might block the traffic, or replies might have no working return route. Checking the listening port is therefore only one step in investigating a failed call. An address or port also provides no proof of permission: reaching a service and being authorised to use it are different requirements. A private network address alone does not establish that a caller is trusted. || Destination name to address / Route packets toward host / Transport selects port 443 / Application accepts a connection

Suppose an application sends HELLO followed by WORLD. With TCP, the receiver gets an ordered stream of bytes. It might read HELLOWO first and RLD later, or read all ten bytes at once. A write by the sender does not establish a message boundary for the receiver. The application needs a framing rule: a way to identify where each message starts and ends. For example, it can send a length before each message and keep reading until that many bytes have arrived. ¶ TCP handles ordering and retransmission to provide a reliable byte stream while the connection works. It can still report a connection failure; it does not promise that the remote application has processed a message. UDP instead provides datagrams, separate units whose boundaries are preserved. It does not supply TCP's reliability and ordering, so an application using it must decide how to handle loss or reordering. ¶ QUIC runs over UDP and provides its own secure transport with separate reliable, ordered streams. Its connection identifiers let peers identify a connection without relying only on its current addresses. Loss affecting one stream need not block delivery on another as a missing TCP byte can. QUIC streams still share congestion constraints, which limit sending to avoid overwhelming the network, and may have application dependencies. Choose a transport by the required behaviour and supported infrastructure. Using UDP does not by itself make an application faster, reliable, or secure. || Application writes messages / TCP exposes ordered bytes / UDP exposes datagrams / QUIC exposes secure streams

A browser knows a name such as example.org, but it needs a usable network address. The Domain Name System, or DNS, supplies records associated with names. A DNS resolver obtains the relevant records, often using cached answers rather than asking authoritative servers, which hold the published records, each time. ¶ A cached answer is a saved result of an earlier lookup. A record's time to live, or TTL, guides how long that result may be cached. While the cached answer remains usable, the resolver can return it without repeating the lookup. This reduces delay and work. It also explains a difficulty during a change: updating the authoritative record does not reach into every cache and replace its saved answer immediately. ¶ Suppose the service moves to another address during failover. Some clients still have the earlier answer; others already have open connections and perform no new lookup. Negative answers, such as a result saying a name does not exist, may also be cached. Changing DNS therefore does not redirect every client immediately. Test DNS refresh and connection lifetimes together when designing failover. The authoritative record is only one part of how clients choose a destination. || Name lookup / Cached answer valid until expiry / Record changes at authority / Existing clients may retain old route

When a browser opens an HTTPS connection, it needs to establish that it is talking to the intended server and protect the traffic. HTTPS uses Transport Layer Security, or TLS, for these purposes. During secure setup, the browser checks the server's certificate, including whether it is valid for the requested hostname under the browser's trust configuration. The peers establish keys for protecting their traffic. ¶ Encryption provides confidentiality: observers without the relevant keys cannot read the protected content. Integrity protection helps detect alteration. Checking the server's identity provides authentication. These functions work together: encrypting a conversation with the wrong server would not meet the browser's original expectation. ¶ Trace where this protection ends. A reverse proxy, which receives requests in front of application servers, may terminate TLS: it handles the secure connection and obtains the unencrypted application messages. Its separate connection to the application needs its own protection policy. The relevant keys also need protection. Finally, the application must check whether a signed-in user may read a particular task. That permission check is authorisation. TLS protects communication with a peer; it does not supply the application's resource ownership rules. || Client validates server identity / Peers establish traffic keys / Encrypted application records / Application still checks authorization

A page often needs several resources, such as a document and two images. HTTP/1.1 clients may use multiple connections to avoid waiting behind other responses. HTTP/2 can carry several streams over one TCP connection. A stream groups the messages belonging to an exchange; multiplexing lets messages from different streams share the connection without finishing one entire response before beginning another. ¶ Sharing TCP also means sharing its ordered byte stream. If a TCP byte is missing, later bytes cannot be delivered to the application until the gap is recovered. Those later bytes may belong to several HTTP/2 streams, so one loss can delay several exchanges. HTTP/3 uses QUIC streams and changes this transport behaviour: a missing part of one stream need not prevent delivery on another. Shared network capacity and application dependencies can still cause waiting. ¶ All three versions retain HTTP's application meanings, such as methods that describe requested actions and status codes that describe responses. They carry messages differently. Performance depends on loss, protocol negotiation, intermediaries, and payloads. Upgrading the protocol can reduce some waiting, but a request can still spend time in application queues and the database. Measure the complete path before attributing a delay to the HTTP version. || Same resource semantics / HTTP/1.1 connection scheduling / HTTP/2 streams over TCP / HTTP/3 streams over QUIC

The first request to a server may need a new connection; a later request can reuse one. A new TCP connection normally performs a handshake, an exchange that establishes connection state at the peers. Secure setup adds exchanges whose number depends on the protocol and reuse options. Existing usable connections avoid some of this work. ¶ We call a request cold when the setup it needs has yet to be done, and warm when it can reuse that earlier work. Suppose the browser receives the task list and then asks for another page through the same usable secure connection. It does not need to establish that connection again. Reuse saves preparation while both ends and the intervening network still regard the connection as usable. ¶ Connection reuse needs limits. An intermediary may close a connection that has been idle, meaning it has carried no traffic for a period. A long-lived connection may also continue using an endpoint that service discovery has replaced. Conversely, closing connections too often repeats setup work and uses more CPU. Choose connection lifetimes and pool sizes that fit the infrastructure and workload. When an old connection fails, replace it, but also consider whether the last request on it could already have taken effect before sending that request again. || Cold request needs setup / Warm request reuses connection / Idle intermediaries may expire it / Pool validates or replaces connection

A round trip is an exchange in which information travels to another participant and a response travels back. Its duration contributes to waiting even if the server's computation is short. If a request must finish one exchange before beginning the next, those waits accumulate. ¶ Assume a simplified cold request spends 20 ms on DNS, 40 ms on TCP setup, 40 ms on TLS setup, and 55 ms on the request, server work, and reply. Draw these as four consecutive intervals. After DNS, 20 ms has elapsed; after TCP setup, 60 ms; after TLS setup, 100 ms. The reply completes at 20 + 40 + 40 + 55 = 155 ms. The 55 ms stage already includes its request and reply travel, so we do not add that travel again. ¶ On the assumed warm path, the necessary lookup and connection setup have already been handled and remain usable. Only the same 55 ms request/work stage remains. This is an arithmetic example, not a benchmark or a universal handshake count. Cached DNS, security resumption, and overlapping work can change the path. Moving a service closer can reduce travel time; reuse or batching can reduce the number of exchanges. First identify which exchanges the actual request needs. || DNS: 20 ms / TCP setup: 40 ms / TLS setup: 40 ms / Request plus work: 55 ms

An organisation may send employees' outbound traffic through a forward proxy. An employee's client contacts that proxy, which makes outbound requests on the client's behalf. A website may instead put a reverse proxy in front of its application servers. A browser sends its request to the service's public endpoint; the reverse proxy receives it and forwards it to a backend server. ¶ Ask whose traffic the proxy represents. The forward proxy serves clients making outbound requests. The reverse proxy presents servers to incoming clients. A reverse proxy can terminate TLS, route requests, and apply limits. Forwarding also creates another communication step: the proxy can fail or time out while the application is still working. ¶ Decide which forwarded metadata the application may trust. Suppose the application accepts an identity header that says “user 7.” If a public caller can supply that header and the application treats it as a checked identity, the caller can impersonate user 7. A trusted proxy must remove or replace public callers’ versions of such headers according to policy and supply verified metadata; the application must know which peers may provide it. The design must also account for callers that can reach the application directly. Trust comes from the enforced communication boundary and verification, rather than the header's name. || Client may choose forward proxy / Reverse proxy fronts application / Proxy forwards selected metadata / Application validates trusted boundary

Suppose incoming requests must be sent to one of three application instances. A load balancer chooses a backend instance to receive traffic. An API gateway often adds functions for an application's external API, such as checking credentials, enforcing quotas, or transforming requests. One product can perform both roles. ¶ Follow a request for task 42. The gateway may check the caller's credential and whether the caller has exceeded an allowance. The balancer then selects an eligible backend. That application checks whether the identified caller may read task 42. These decisions use different information: the traffic allowance can be a boundary policy, while task ownership depends on application records. The same distinction applies to ownership of a payment. ¶ Assign responsibilities explicitly instead of relying on product names. Keep each required rule enforced where the information needed to make the decision is available. Moving every business rule into the gateway makes many teams depend on changes there. Also trace downstream capacity. If all three application instances use the same overloaded database, distributing requests among more instances does not remove that database's limit. A backend that is running may still lack the capacity to accept more useful work. || Edge receives request / Gateway applies boundary policy / Balancer chooses healthy backend / Domain service enforces resource rules

To route a connection using addresses and transport ports, a balancer can work with transport information. This is called Layer-4 routing, after the transport layer in a common model of network protocols. To route an HTTP request by its host or path, it must inspect application messages. This is Layer-7 routing, after the application layer in that model. ¶ Compare two rules. “Send connections on this port to this backend pool” needs no HTTP path. “Send /images to the image service and /tasks to the task service” requires the request to be visible. If HTTP is encrypted, the routing component needs an arrangement that lets it inspect the relevant messages, commonly by terminating the encrypted connection. That also gives the component responsibility for handling the decrypted content. ¶ Application-aware routing supports richer decisions but adds parsing, processing, and trust requirements. Also distinguish requests from connections. A Layer-4 balancer generally selects a backend for a connection; a long-lived connection can remain attached to that backend while new connections go elsewhere. A Layer-7 proxy can make decisions about HTTP requests, but an established stream still needs a continuous delivery path. Choose the routing level by the information the decision needs and the connection behaviour the application expects. || L4: address and transport port / L7: host, path, headers / Select backend under policy / Existing stream retains affinity

Assume 1,000 clients ask for updates every five seconds. This repeated asking is polling. It produces an average of 1,000 / 5 = 200 requests per second even when nothing changes. Each response supplies another snapshot. Clients polling together can also create bursts around that average, and an update may wait until the next poll to be seen. ¶ With Server-Sent Events, or SSE, the server sends a stream of events to the client over HTTP. With WebSockets, either end can send application messages over the established channel. A server can now send a task update when it becomes available, avoiding repeated empty polls. In return, the system keeps long-lived connection state and buffers. Polling can also reuse connections, so the saving is primarily repeated requests and responses; how much setup it saves depends on reuse. ¶ Plan for a client that disconnects after receiving event 20. If events 21–23 occur before it returns, opening a new connection alone does not recover them. The application needs retained event identities and a replay rule, or a fresh snapshot that restores current state. Heartbeats help check whether an idle path is still usable, and buffer limits prevent a slow client from consuming unbounded memory. A live connection provides a delivery path; durable update history requires a separate arrangement. || 1000 clients / Poll every 5 seconds / Baseline: 200 requests/s / Streaming trades polls for connection state

A client opens an outbound connection, but no reply arrives. The problem may lie outside the remote application. A firewall applies traffic rules and may block the request or reply. Network address translation, or NAT, rewrites addressing as traffic crosses a boundary, often allowing clients with private addresses to communicate through a shared public address. ¶ A reply must find its way back as well as the request finding its way out. When NAT rewrites a source address and port, it keeps a mapping so returning traffic can reach the original client. If that mapping expires while a connection is idle, the application may still hold a socket even though the return path no longer works as expected. Exhausting the available mappings can also prevent new communication. ¶ Ingress means traffic entering a chosen boundary; egress means traffic leaving it. A request is egress from the client's network and ingress to the server's network, so name the boundary when using these terms. Trace both directions through routing, NAT, and firewall rules. Choose connection lifetimes and outbound policies together. Dividing the network into segments can limit which services are reachable, but an allowed connection must still authenticate and authorise its requests, particularly when several tenants share a system. || Outbound socket allocated / NAT mapping and firewall policy / Remote response returns / Expired state can break the path

An application wants to call “the payment service” even as individual instances are replaced. Service discovery maps that logical name to usable endpoints, such as addresses and ports. It may use DNS, a registry that records available instances, or the platform's control plane, which manages deployment information. ¶ Suppose discovery publishes instances A and B. The client saves that list and chooses A, but A stops before the request arrives. The list was correct when it was produced; it could not promise that A would remain available. Selection, connection failure, refreshing the list, and choosing another endpoint are separate steps in the request path. Even a running instance may already lack spare capacity. ¶ Caching discovery results avoids making every call wait for a lookup, but those results need deliberate refresh rules. A client must also cope with a bad endpoint within its overall deadline. Retrying another instance is appropriate only when the operation's retry contract allows it: failure to receive a reply does not necessarily mean the first instance performed no work. Successful discovery establishes a possible destination. It does not establish that the operation will succeed or that repeating it will be safe. || Instance registers / Discovery publishes endpoints / Client selects cached endpoint / Failure requires bounded refresh and retry

A user requests a public image. A content delivery network, or CDN, can serve it from a nearby edge location, a serving location closer to the user than the original source. On a cache miss, the edge fetches the image from the origin server and stores a copy according to its caching policy. A later visitor may receive that copy without another origin fetch. ¶ The cache key decides whether two requests refer to the same stored representation. For example, two image sizes may need different keys so that a request for a large image does not receive the thumbnail. Freshness rules decide when the copy may be reused and when it needs checking or replacement. A hit avoids some travel and origin work; a miss still needs the origin path. ¶ These are also correctness decisions. If content varies by tenant or permission, sharing a copy under a key that omits that distinction can expose someone else's content. Including a tenant identifier is not a substitute for checking access. Public images and private account pages need different policies, including whether shared caching is permitted at all. CDNs reduce some network travel and origin traffic while adding copies whose expiry, purge, and failure behaviour must be understood. || Browser requests asset / Nearby edge checks cache key / Miss fetches origin / Cached representation has freshness policy

A client sends a request and waits until its deadline, the latest time it is willing to wait. It then reports a timeout. Did the server receive the request, perform it, or commit its write? The timeout alone cannot answer these questions. The request or its reply may have been delayed or lost. ¶ Consider two histories. In one, the request never reaches the server. In the other, the server commits and only the reply is lost. The client sees the same timeout in both. Recovery must work despite this missing information. An operation identifier helps only when the server uses it to recognise repeated work or return the stored result. A supported status query can also supply evidence. Blindly repeating a charge as a new operation would risk a second charge in the second history. ¶ A network partition prevents some participants from communicating even while other paths work. Individual lost packets may be recovered by a transport, but a request can still run out of time. Distinguish a connection-setup limit, an idle limit on inactivity, and an overall request deadline. Unbounded waiting holds resources; starting a fresh full timeout for every retry can also make total waiting unbounded. Keep attempts within the overall deadline and retain an uncertain outcome until evidence resolves it. || Request may reach server / Server may commit / Reply may be lost / Caller sees only a timeout

A database connection pool keeps connections that requests can borrow and return. Suppose it contains twenty connections, and each is held for an assumed average of 50 ms. Since 50 ms is 0.05 seconds, one continuously occupied connection would serve about 1 / 0.05 = 20 operations per second. Twenty such connections give 20 / 0.05 = 400 operations per second before overhead and variability, assuming one operation per borrowing period. ¶ This is an ideal calculation under the stated hold time, not a measured database limit. While all twenty connections are borrowed, another request waits for a return even though it needs no new connection setup. Include this acquisition wait in its deadline. Longer hold times reduce the rate: a request that keeps a connection while waiting for another service occupies a slot throughout that wait. ¶ Increasing the pool helps only if the database can use the extra concurrent work effectively. It may instead increase contention and hold times. A leaked connection, which is never returned, gradually reduces usable capacity. Keepalive probes can help detect some broken connections; they do not establish whether a business operation succeeded or whether the database has spare capacity. Measure acquisition waits and hold times before changing the pool size, and ensure borrowed connections are released when work succeeds, fails, or is cancelled. || Pool capacity: 20 / Mean hold time: 0.05 s / Ideal ceiling: 400 operations/s / Queue and timeout before exhaustion''')

put(4, '''Open two copies of the same upload program. Both start from the same instructions on disk, but each has its own running state. The instructions form a program; each running instance is a process. A process has an address space in which its code and data are available, together with resources such as open files and connections. ¶ Inside a process, threads provide separate paths of execution. They commonly share heap memory and process resources, while each has its own stack and execution state. One thread can therefore use a buffer that another thread created. That convenience also lets one thread interfere with data another is using. ¶ These runtime boundaries differ from source files and modules. Several modules can execute within one process, and a process crash can stop all its threads. Separate processes provide useful isolation, although they can still depend on the same storage or machine. To understand failure and resource ownership, identify the running processes and threads as well as the organisation of the source code. || Program on disk / Process owns address space / Threads share process resources / Process failure removes its threads

When an upload handler calls a function, the runtime keeps information about the call on a stack. This commonly includes local execution information and where to resume after the function returns. Dynamically managed objects, such as an upload buffer, commonly live on the heap. The exact placement and management depend on the language and runtime. ¶ Suppose the handler creates a buffer, passes it to a background task, and returns. Its function call has ended, but the background task still needs the bytes. In a garbage-collected runtime, a retained reference can keep that buffer reachable and prevent its reclamation. Other memory-management arrangements must also keep the bytes valid until the task finishes using them. Counting only active function calls would miss this continuing memory use. ¶ Follow who retains each resource and when that responsibility ends. A task may keep a buffer, an open file, and a database connection while it waits. Garbage collection of memory does not by itself establish timely closure of external resources. Give those resources an explicit cleanup path, and bound the number and size of outstanding uploads. || Request allocates buffer / Task retains reference / Request returns / Buffer remains until ownership ends

On one core, two ready threads take turns. The scheduler chooses a thread to run. When execution switches to another thread, the runtime and operating system preserve the execution state needed to resume the first and restore the state of the next. This is a context switch. Switching can also disturb useful CPU-cache contents. ¶ Suppose thread A starts a disk read and blocks while it waits. The scheduler can let ready thread B use the core during that interval. A busy-wait loop behaves differently: it keeps executing checks, consuming CPU time while the result remains unavailable. ¶ More threads can help when some spend time waiting and others have useful work for an idle CPU. If the CPU is already saturated with computation, additional runnable threads mostly have to wait their turn and can add switching overhead. Inspect CPU utilisation, runnable queues, and lock contention before choosing a thread count. Starting one thread for every incoming request leaves thread and resource use free to grow with demand. || Runnable work queues / CPU runs one context / Context switch restores another / Too many runnable tasks add overhead

Assume 1,000 independent uploads each spend 100 ms waiting for storage and require negligible CPU work. Handling them sequentially gives 1,000 × 100 ms = 100,000 ms, or 100 seconds, of waiting. Allowing fifty waits at once divides the uploads into twenty ideal groups: 20 × 100 ms = 2 seconds. This is a waiting-time lower bound under the assumed overlap, before overhead, bandwidth limits, or slower storage under load. ¶ Most time in this example passes while storage performs work elsewhere. Overlapping independent waits can use an otherwise idle interval. We call the workload I/O-bound when I/O is its main limit. Compression may instead be CPU-bound: a core must execute the compression instructions, so useful computing capacity becomes the limit. ¶ Extra asynchronous tasks do not create extra CPU cores. For computation, investigate algorithms and actual parallel execution. For waiting, concurrency can help if storage and connection capacity support it. A real upload may combine both kinds of work; measure which resource limits the workload at the concurrency level being considered. || 1000 uploads / 100 ms assumed wait each / Sequential wait sum: 100 s / 50 concurrent waits: about 2 s ideal

Imagine asking the upload service to prepare a report. A synchronous interface returns the result, or an error, before the caller proceeds past the call. An asynchronous interface represents initiation and later completion separately. It may supply a handle for the eventual result, allowing the caller to arrange other work before waiting for it. ¶ Now ask a different question: what happens to the executing thread while the operation waits? An asynchronous function can still call a blocking library routine. If that routine waits on the only thread serving an event loop, other requests on that loop cannot run during the wait. Long computation on the same thread has a similar effect. ¶ The interface tells us how a caller receives completion; it does not establish how every underlying operation uses a thread. Trace the actual execution path, including drivers and libraries, when choosing an execution model. We will next examine how nonblocking I/O lets a thread make progress on other tasks while an operation is unfinished. || Start operation / Caller may await completion / Underlying call may still block / Inspect runtime and driver behavior

Suppose an event-loop thread tries to read an upload from a network connection. With a blocking read, the calling thread can remain inside that call until data or another completion condition arrives. A nonblocking read can instead report that no bytes are ready yet. The program arranges to be notified when progress is possible, and the thread can run another ready task during the wait. ¶ The platform may report readiness, meaning that the program should try the operation again, or report completion of a submitted operation. Follow the intended platform interface: a notification does not always mean that the whole upload has arrived. Repeatedly checking an unfinished operation without yielding merely turns the wait into CPU work. ¶ If a necessary library blocks, the application may run its calls on a separate executor rather than on the event-loop thread. Bound both the active calls and the jobs waiting for that executor. Also define cancellation and cleanup: moving a wait to another thread changes which thread is occupied, but the outstanding operation still holds resources. || Submit I/O / Register readiness or completion / Run another ready task / Resume when operation can progress

Imagine two uploads on one core. Upload A starts, then waits for storage. The core works on B during that wait, and later resumes A. Their lifetimes overlap, so both activities are in progress during the same period. This is concurrency. ¶ If two cores execute A and B at the same instant, there is parallelism as well. Concurrency concerns overlapping progress; parallelism concerns simultaneous execution. The one-core timeline already provides concurrency, even though only one thread executes instructions at a time. ¶ Interleaving can still affect correctness on one core. A may read a counter and pause before writing its new value. B can read and change that counter before A resumes. Two instructions need not run simultaneously for their operations to interfere. Decide separately how many operations may be in progress, how much work can execute at once, and how shared updates are coordinated. These choices govern different parts of the design. || Task A starts / Task A waits while B runs / Activities overlap in time / Multiple cores can execute simultaneously

An event loop runs ready pieces of work in turn. Suppose an upload task reaches an await for an unfinished read. A future or promise represents the eventual result, according to the runtime's rules. The task can suspend at that point, allowing the loop to run other ready work. When the read completes, the suspended task's continuation—the code that will resume it—becomes eligible to run. ¶ Completion and execution are separate events. The result may be ready while another callback still occupies the loop. A callback that computes continuously for two seconds on the only event-loop thread makes other ready tasks wait for those two seconds. Merely creating more futures cannot provide another executing thread or core. ¶ Keep long computation away from the loop or divide it into bounded turns where the runtime permits. Await behaviour also depends on the runtime and whether the result is already available; an await need not always suspend. Finally, scheduling a task does not make it durable. A future and its task held only in memory can disappear when their process dies. || Task awaits future / Loop runs ready work / Completion makes continuation ready / Long callback delays other tasks

Suppose eight workers process uploads. Each worker takes one job, processes it, and then becomes available for another. The pool limits active jobs to eight. Yet accepting a thousand large buffers into a waiting queue can still exhaust memory before those jobs reach a worker. ¶ Separate the workers from the waiting room. A worker limit controls how many jobs execute now; a queue limit controls how many wait to execute later. If every queued item owns a large buffer, the queue may consume more memory than the active workers. Where practical, store upload bytes separately and queue compact references. Count the active buffers and open resources as well as the waiting references. ¶ Decide what happens when the queue is full: reject the work, wait until a deadline, or use another supported path. Compact references save queue memory but do not make a process-local queue recoverable. If acceptance promises that processing will survive process loss, record the job durably so it can be found again, rather than relying only on the workers' in-memory queue. || Bounded input queue / 8 workers execute jobs / Queue stores object references / Full queue triggers admission policy

Two threads need to reduce the quantity in an inventory map. In a shared-memory arrangement, both can access the map directly. They must use an agreed coordination rule so that one update does not interfere with another. ¶ Alternatively, one owner can keep the map private. The other tasks send commands such as “reserve one item,” and the owner processes those commands against its state. This is message passing. When the owner handles each command in turn, callers no longer perform competing direct updates to the map. Coordination has moved to the owner's command processing and message delivery. ¶ Ownership must be real: sending a pointer to an object that both sides can still mutate has not transferred exclusive access. Nor does message passing settle every failure. A command can repeat, a reply can be lost, and the owner can fail. Define how those events are handled and how state is recovered. Choose the arrangement by where state belongs and how much communication the work requires; both approaches need clear access and failure rules. || Shared counter needs coordination / Owner receives commands / Owner updates private state / Messages can still repeat or fail

Start a counter at zero. A reads zero into its local calculation. Before A writes, B also reads zero. A adds one and writes one; B adds one to the value it read earlier and also writes one. Two intended increments have left the counter at one. This is a lost update. ¶ The outcome depends on the interleaving of operations, so this is a race condition. Protecting only the final assignments would still allow both callers to calculate from zero. The critical section—the part that must be protected from interfering access—must include the complete read–modify–write sequence. With a suitable shared lock, A reads and writes first; B then reads one and writes two. A supported atomic increment can provide the required indivisible update too. ¶ Every relevant accessor must follow the chosen coordination rule. Separate locks belonging to A and B do not exclude each other, and a process-local mutex cannot protect a database from unrelated processes. This trace illustrates a compound-update failure; actual unsynchronised memory access must be judged by the language's memory model, which may impose stronger restrictions than this simple interleaving suggests. || A reads 0 / B reads 0 / A writes 1 / B writes 1: increment lost

Choose a coordination tool by what callers must be allowed to do. A mutex permits one holder to enter a protected section at a time. Both counter callers can acquire the same mutex around their read, calculation, and write. A semaphore instead manages a number of permits. With four permits, at most four uploads can hold a scarce resource; later callers wait or fail under the admission policy. Four permits would not make a shared counter update exclusive. ¶ A condition variable lets a thread wait for a condition in shared state. Suppose a worker finds the queue empty while holding its mutex. Waiting releases that mutex so a producer can add a job, then reacquires it before the worker proceeds. The worker checks “is the queue nonempty?” again in a loop. A wake-up alone is insufficient: another worker may have taken the job, and some interfaces allow wake-ups without a relevant state change. ¶ An atomic operation makes its specified action indivisible under defined memory-order rules. Separate atomic actions do not automatically form one combined operation. A transfer changes two balances together, so its protection must cover the relationship between them as well as each individual value. || Acquire lock / Check predicate in loop / Wait releases and reacquires lock / Change state and notify appropriately

A holds the inventory lock and waits for the payment lock. B holds the payment lock and waits for inventory. Neither releases its current lock because neither can finish the work it is waiting to perform. This circular wait is a deadlock. ¶ Draw an arrow from each waiting participant to the owner it needs. A points to B and B points to A, forming a cycle. Requiring every caller to acquire inventory before payment removes this particular cycle: a caller cannot hold payment while trying to acquire inventory in the opposite order. The ordering rule must apply to every relevant path. ¶ Two other progress failures behave differently. In starvation, a participant repeatedly misses the resource or service it needs while others proceed. In livelock, participants remain active but make no useful progress, as when both repeatedly yield and retry in the same pattern. Shorter critical sections or different ownership may help. Timeouts also need cleanup and rollback; retrying the same failed arrangement indefinitely does not establish progress. || A holds inventory lock / B holds payment lock / A waits for payment / B waits for inventory: cycle

A writer constructs an object containing an upload title, then makes a reference available to another thread. We intend the reader to use the completed title. Establishing that the writer ran earlier on a clock is insufficient: the language's memory model determines which writes the reader is guaranteed to observe. ¶ Use a publication mechanism with the required synchronisation guarantees. For example, the writer can initialise the object and publish it through an appropriately used lock or channel; the reader performs the corresponding synchronising operation before using it. The exact guarantee depends on that language and mechanism. Simply setting an ordinary shared “ready” flag is not a general substitute. ¶ Happens-before is an ordering relation used by memory models to express guarantees about the visibility and ordering of effects. It describes a specified relationship between events, rather than merely their timestamps. The Go memory model is one concrete set of such rules; other languages must be checked against their own specifications. Safe publication matters even if nobody will modify the object after construction: readers still need to see its initialised state. || Writer initializes object / Synchronizing publish operation / Reader synchronizes receive / Reader observes specified initialized state

Suppose a task record never changes after construction. A and B can read its fields without competing to modify them. Such a value is immutable. Sharing immutable values removes many opportunities for conflicting writes, once the values have been safely made available to readers. ¶ Now put those records in a mutable dictionary. Adding a second record changes the dictionary even though neither record changes. Concurrent insertions, removals, or iteration still need the guarantees of an appropriate container or coordination mechanism. We must identify exactly which value is immutable and which surrounding operations remain mutable. ¶ Thread safety is a claim about the allowed concurrent use of a whole object or operation. State who may read or change it and what protects that access. Keeping data inside one owner, copying values, and using locks are other ways to establish suitable rules. Returning a writable internal collection allows callers to bypass those rules. Immutable records alone cannot protect that surrounding collection or the way new records are published. || Construct immutable value / Publish through defined boundary / Readers do not mutate value / Mutable container still needs coordination

Assume jobs arrive at 150 per second while workers complete 100 per second. If every job is accepted and none is abandoned, unfinished work increases by 150 − 100 = 50 jobs each second. With the workers already busy, this excess accumulates in the waiting queue. After ten seconds, the mismatch has added 50 × 10 = 500 waiting jobs under those assumptions. ¶ A larger queue gives the service more room to wait; it leaves the extra fifty arrivals per second unchanged. Continued growth stops only if the service reduces accepted arrivals, increases useful completions, or changes the work required. A bounded queue makes it possible to apply an admission policy before resources are exhausted. ¶ Backpressure slows or limits upstream work according to downstream capacity. Use bounded queues, admission deadlines, and clear overload responses so producers can react. Keep queued payloads compact, and monitor the age of the oldest job as well as the number waiting. When a dependency slows, unchecked concurrency can make more tasks retain buffers, threads, and connections throughout the longer wait. || Arrivals: 150 jobs/s / Completion: 100 jobs/s / Backlog grows: 50 jobs/s / Bound queue and reject excess work

An upload request starts a file read and a conversion task. If its deadline expires, both tasks may still be running and holding resources. Structured concurrency gives related child tasks a parent scope responsible for their completion, failure, and lifetime. The scope keeps track of the children instead of letting them silently outlive their owner. ¶ When the caller disconnects, the scope can request cancellation of work that is no longer needed. Children must reach the runtime's supported stopping points and release their resources; the parent waits for their permitted termination before the scope ends. Cancellation is a request with operation-specific behaviour, so an unresponsive child may delay that cleanup. Use reliable cleanup paths for buffers, files, and connections. ¶ Stopping local work does not establish that a remote effect was undone. A payment may have committed before cancellation arrived, requiring a status check or reconciliation. Work deliberately accepted for later completion needs an owner beyond the request: persist its job identity, input reference, and progress. Then cancellation of the connection can release request resources without losing the accepted processing obligation. || Parent request starts children / Deadline cancels owned tasks / Cleanup releases local resources / Reconcile external effects separately''')
